[standards/application/styling/STD-APP-UI-001-frontend-styling-standards.md#std-ui-007-use-cn-for-conditional-classes]

#### STD-UI-007: Use `cn()` for Conditional Classes

**MUST** use the `cn()` utility (which wraps `clsx` and `tailwind-merge`) for all conditional class application. Do not use string concatenation or ternary expressions that may produce duplicate or conflicting Tailwind classes.

```typescript
// src/lib/utils.ts — standard utility
import { clsx, type ClassValue } from 'clsx';
import { twMerge } from 'tailwind-merge';

export function cn(...inputs: ClassValue[]) {
  return twMerge(clsx(inputs));
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ COMPLIANT — cn() merges and de-duplicates classes correctly
<button
  className={cn(
    'inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium',
    'focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring',
    variant === 'destructive' && 'bg-destructive text-destructive-foreground',
    variant === 'outline' && 'border border-input bg-background',
    disabled && 'cursor-not-allowed opacity-50',
    className
  )}
>
  {children}
</button>

// ❌ NON-COMPLIANT — string concatenation creates merge conflicts
<button className={`bg-primary ${variant === 'outline' ? 'bg-background' : ''}`}>
```

